<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="ch9-section-isobaric-coordinates-subsection-pgf" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Pressure gradient acceleration</title>

    <p>
        Terms in the equations of motion representing the horizontal components of the pressure gradient acceleration
        were found by holding <m>z</m> constant and allowing <m>p</m> to vary.
        They must be rewritten now that <m>p</m> is held constant in isobaric coordinates while <m>z</m> varies.
    </p>

    <p>
        We replace <m>f</m> with <m>p</m>
        in <xref ref="eqn-chain-rule-x-p-reduced" text="type-global" />
        to write the <m>x</m>-component of the pressure gradient acceleration in isobaric coordinates.
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-chain-rule-p-p-reduced" number="yes">
            \left(
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial x}
            \right)_{p}
            =
            \left(
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial x}
            \right)_{z}
            +
            \left(
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial z}
            \right)_{x}
            \left(
                \frac{\partial z}{\partial x}
            \right)_{p}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        But <m>\left( \dfrac{\partial p}{\partial x} \right)_{p} = 0</m> since <m>p</m> does not change on an isobaric surface,
        and so <xref ref="eqn-chain-rule-p-p-reduced" text="type-global" /> reduces to
    </p>

    <p>
        <md number="yes">
            0
            =
            \left(
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial x}
            \right)_{z}
            +
            \left(
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial z}
            \right)_{x}
            \left(
                \frac{\partial z}{\partial x}
            \right)_{p}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        This rearranges to produce the relationship
    </p>

    <p>
        <md number="yes">
            \left(
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial x}
            \right)_{z}
            =
            -\left(
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial z}
            \right)_{x}
            \left(
                \frac{\partial z}{\partial x}
            \right)_{p}
        </md>
    </p>

</subsection>